<figure><img src="https://baty.net/img/2026/20260928-baty-blog-editor.webp" alt="Screenshot of the Mac app"></figure><p>I didn't want the Baty.net Editor to be just another tab buried in my browser, so I had Claude make me a real Mac app out of it.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>In a normal world, the only way I'd be able to split a website into its own app would be &quot;Add to Dock&quot; via Safari. That would work, but it would mean that running the local Node server for Eleventy was a separate step from the app. It would also only have Safari's default menus.</p>
<p>I wanted something nicer, so I had Claude build me a Swift wrapper. I suppose I could have used something like Electron, but my Mac Mini only has 16 GB of memory, so Electron would be too heavy.</p>
<p>It took Claude Code 15 minutes to wrap the thing, create the app, add an icon, write the readme, and commit all of it. What a world.</p>
<p><img src="https://baty.net/img/2026/20260928-my-applications.webp" alt="App screenshot" /></p>
<p>When I launch the app, it starts the server automatically. Also, there are actual menus.</p>
<p><img src="https://baty.net/img/2026/20260928-menus.webp" alt="App screenshot showing File menu" /></p>
<p>It's currently using only 99 MB of memory. This stuff makes me a little giddy.</p>
